plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 I have produced a pair of programs which overcome most of these problems, and in particular automate the production and use of Style Maps with sensible mappings.\par \pard \plain \s17 \sb40 \sa280 \sl280 \keepn \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f8 \fs28 \b 2\tab The Problems\par \pard \plain \s18 \sb40 \sa240 \sl240 \keepn \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f8 \b 2.1\tab Character Sets\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 The Macintosh uses a different character set to the Series 3. Characters with codes less than 128 (decimal), i.e. the ASCII set, are identical. Other characters are different. Sometimes a character in one set has an equivalent in the other set, but with a different code. In other cases there is no equivalent. The characters in question include all the accented characters, special punctuation characters, as well as that extremely useful (for us Brits!) pound sign.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 In theory the RTF format should cater for this. Non ASCII characters are represented in the form "\\'hh", where hh is the two digit hexadecimal value of its code in the originating character set. The header of the RTF file includes an indication of the origin of the file; e.g. "\\pca" for the Series 3, "\\mac" for the Macintosh. This should enable the destination program to convert the non ASCII characters to their local equivalent.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 In practice the version of Word I use on my Macintosh (v4) only manages to convert correctly characters with codes between 128 and 137. Other characters are converted to a default value. This may be a reflection of the fact there is no 'standard' PC character set.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 The Series 3 appears to convert all Macintosh characters correctly. At least Psion got that bit right!\par \pard \plain \s18 \sb40 \sa240 \sl240 \keepn \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f8 \b 2.2\tab Font Mapping\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 Font definitions in RTF have two parts: a \plain \f26 \i family\plain \f26  and a \plain \f26 \i name\plain \f26 . E.g. \plain \f26 \b froman Times\plain \f26 , where \plain \f26 \b froman \plain \f26 is the \plain \f26 \i family\plain \f26  and \plain \f26 \b Times\plain \f26  the \plain \f26 \i  name\plain \f26 , or \plain \f26 \b fswiss Helvetica\plain \f26 , where \plain \f26 \b fswiss\plain \f26  is the \plain \f26 \i family\plain \f26  and \plain \f26 \b Helvetica\plain \f26  the \plain \f26 \i name\plain \f26 .\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 When reading font definitions Word on the Series 3 looks for an exact match between the \plain \f26 \i family/name\plain \f26  combination and the valid combinations defined in the currently set printer driver. If there is no match then it assigns a known font with a matching \plain \f26 \i family\plain \f26 .\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 Unfortunately the \plain \f26 \i family/name\plain \f26  combinations generated by my Mac (configured for printing to an Apple Laserwriter) do not correspond to those defined in the Series 3 Apple Laserwriter driver. For example the Mac outputs \plain \f26 \b fnil Palatino\plain \f26 , while the Series 3 expects \plain \f26 \b \ul froman\plain \f26 \b  Palatino\plain \f26  and assigns \plain \f26 \b Courier\plain \f26  instead!\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 The Mac appears to match on \plain \f26 \i name\plain \f26  rather than \plain \f26 \i family\plain \f26 , e.g. incoming \plain \f26 \b froman Palatino\plain \f26  is mapped onto \plain \f26 \b Palatino\plain \f26 , far more logical and useful. Psion 1, Apple 1!\par \pard \plain \s18 \sb40 \sa240 \sl240 \keepn \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f8 \b 2.3\tab Style Names\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 When reading RTF style definitions Word on the Series 3 attempts to derive a short code for the style from the first two characters of the style's name. However if this doesn't form a valid and unique code then a default (meaningless) code is assigned. At least the real name is now preserved in all cases, as the "full" name.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 When generating an output RTF style sheet then only the short code is output, except for certain 'standard' styles, e.g. \plain \f26 \b Body Text\plain \f26 .\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 The result is that if you export a document to the Series 3 to work on, and then copy it back to the Mac, all the original style names will be 'lost'. The document's style sheet will be full of meaningless names such as \plain \f26 \b ZA\plain \f26 , etc.\par \pard \plain \s17 \sb40 \sa280 \sl280 \keepn \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f8 \fs28 \b 3\tab The Solutions\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 The solution I have adopted for the problem with non ASCII characters is as follows:\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 I convert the hexadecimal representation of the character to the actual character itself, in the Series 3 character set. I then rely on the conversions carried out by the MCLINK program to convert it to the corresponding character in the Mac character set. I also change the RTF header to make it appear that the RTF file originated on the Mac, to suppress any further conversion by Mac Word.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 The problem with fonts is now readily solved by setting up a suitable style and font mapping file. See the documentation distributed with the latest RTF drivers (available from 3-Lib in RTF220.ZIP).\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 The difficulties with style names are also overcome using a mapping file, which is generated automatically for each RTF file to be imported to the Series 3. A more intelligent algorithm is used to ensure a meaningful short code is generated for virtually any style name. This tries the following combinations in order, until a previously unused code is formed.\par \pard \plain \s16 \qj \fi-360 \li720 \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \f26 \'FA\tab If the style name contains more than one word (space separated), the first character of the first and second words;\par \pard \plain \s16 \qj \fi-360 \li720 \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \f26 \'FA\tab The first and second characters of the style name;\par \pard \plain \s16 \qj \fi-360 \li720 \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \f26 \'FA\tab Then the first and third characters, etc.;\par \pard \plain \s16 \qj \fi-360 \li720 \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \f26 \'FA\tab Finally, if all else fails, the combinations QA, QB, etc.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 In all cases lower case letters are converted to upper case, and the digits \plain \f26 \b 1\plain \f26  to \plain \f26 \b 9\plain \f26  to letters \plain \f26 \b A\plain \f26  to \plain \f26 \b I\plain \f26 .\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 The outline level is set as follows:\par \pard \plain \s16 \qj \fi-360 \li720 \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \f26 \'FA\tab If the style name is of the form \plain \f26 \b HEAD... n\plain \f26 , n = 1 .. 9 (ignoring case): n;\par \pard \plain \s16 \qj \fi-360 \li720 \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \f26 \'FA\tab If the style name is of the form \plain \f26 \b HEAD...: \plain \f26 1;\par \pard \plain \s16 \qj \fi-360 \li720 \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \f26 \'FA\tab Otherwise: 9.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 This mapping file is saved for use when the document is transferred back to the Mac. This then ensures the style definitions are output to the RTF file with their correct full names.\par \pard \plain \s17 \sb40 \sa280 \sl280 \keepn \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f8 \fs28 \b 4\tab The Programs\par \pard \plain \s18 \sb40 \sa240 \sl240 \keepn \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f8 \b 4.1\tab Installation and Preparation\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 There are two versions of each program, for the Series 3 and Series 3A. The only difference is the Series 3A has a fancier progress bar using the grey plane.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 For the Series 3 copy RTFMACS3.OPA and MACRTFS3.OPA to \\APP on any of the Series 3's drives. For the Series 3A similarly copy RTFMAC3A.OPA and MACRTF3A.OPA. Then install these applications as normal, using \plain \f26 \b Psion-I\plain \f26  on the System screen. You may find it helpful to assign a button to RTFMAC.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 You must also set up a default mapping file: RTFMAP.DEF, in \\WDR on the internal drive M:. This should contain the mapping between the font names generated on the Mac and corresponding font ids used by the Series 3.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 I have included the file I use, which I have configured for the fonts I commonly use on an Apple Laserwriter. If you wish to set up your own font mappings then you need to find out the \plain \f26 \i family/name\plain \f26  combinations which your Mac generates. Do this by saving a document in RTF format from Word on the Mac, and then examining the resultant file. The font definitions are in a block beginning "\{\\fontbl...", which contains entries of the form "\{\\f\plain \f26 \i id\plain \f26  \plain \f26 \i family name\plain \f26 ;\}". The corresponding ids used by the Series 3 can be obtained from the table in the documentation with the latest RTF drivers.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 You must also ensure that the file \plain \f26 \b MCLINK.EXT\plain \f26  (in the same Mac folder as the MCLINK program) contains the extension ".RTF". This ensures MCLINK treats RTF file as text files, and will perform the conversion between character sets.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 Note the conversion table (in file \plain \f26 \b MCLINK.XLT\plain \f26 ) supplied with my Serial Link did not have entries to convert the Series 3 bullet characters (codes 250 and 254) to the equivalent Mac character (code 165). It would be worth checking this in your table.\par \pard \plain \s18 \sb40 \sa240 \sl240 \keepn \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f8 \b 4.2\tab Importing from Mac to Series 3\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 On the Mac save the file in RTF format. Connect the Series 3 to the Mac using the Serial Link, and establish the connection in the usual way.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 Activate the \plain \f26 \b MACRTF\plain \f26  application, by moving the highlight on System screen beneath its icon and pressing \plain \f26 \b Enter\plain \f26 . A dialog will now be displayed for you to select the RTF file to be imported from the Mac and the destination (normally \\WRD on one of the Series 3's drives). The application remembers the Mac disk and folder you imported the previous file from, and sets this as the default path for the file selector.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 If you complete the dialog with \plain \f26 \b Enter\plain \f26  the Application carries out the following actions:\par \pard \plain \s16 \qj \fi-360 \li720 \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \f26 \'FA\tab Copies the file unchanged from the Mac to the specified destination directory;\par \pard \plain \s16 \qj \fi-360 \li720 \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \f26 \'FA\tab Creates a mapping file (in the destination directory, with same name as the RTF file but extension ".STY") containing font mappings from RTFMAP.DEF and style mappings generated as described above;\par \pard \plain \s16 \qj \fi-360 \li720 \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \f26 \'FA\tab Copies this mapping file to M:\\WDR\\RTFMAP.TXT, ready for Word to use when translating the RTF file.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 You should now open the RTF file from Word on the Series 3. You should then find all the fonts have been set correctly and styles have been given 'sensible' short codes.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 Note that if you do not open the file immediately, and in the meantime perform further operations using either MACRTF or RTFMAC, then the mapping file set up as M:\\WDR\\RTFMAP.TXT will no longer be the correct one. If this is the case then you should manually copy \plain \f26 \i filename\plain \f26 .STY to M:\\WDR\\RTFMAP.TXT.\par \pard \plain \s18 \sb40 \sa240 \sl240 \keepn \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f8 \b 4.3\tab Exporting from Series 3 to Macintosh\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 Note the RTFMAC program runs in two stages. The first stage sets up the correct mapping file for Word's RTF translator to use. The second transfers the file to the Mac. Between these two stages the document is saved from Word as an RTF file.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 The RTFMAC program lists all \plain \f26 \b Word\plain \f26  documents beneath its icon; you should highlight the document you wish to export and type \plain \f26 \b Enter\plain \f26  to start the program.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 A dialog is then displayed for you to select the document to be exported and the corresponding style mapping file. For the latter you can choose:\par \pard \plain \s16 \qj \fi-360 \li720 \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \f26 \'FA\tab  a file with a specified name; \par \pard \plain \s16 \qj \fi-360 \li720 \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \f26 \'FA\tab the default file (containing your font mappings only); \par \pard \plain \s16 \qj \fi-360 \li720 \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \f26 \'FA\tab or no mapping file at all.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 If the program finds a mapping file to match the selected Word document (i.e. same name and ".STY" extension) then it offers this. Otherwise it sets the "Use Style Map" to "Default". You can use the (lower) file selector to choose another mapping file; this would normally be the mapping file generated when the original document from which the current document has been derived was imported from the Mac. (The upper file selector specifies the Word document which is being exported.)\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 After you complete this dialog with \plain \f26 \b Enter\plain \f26  the program copies the specified mapping file to M:\\WDR\\RTFMAP.TXT, unless you selected no mapping file, in which case any existing M:\\WDR\\RTFMAP.TXT is deleted. The second dialog is now displayed, for you to select the destination.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 However DO NOT complete this dialog, but switch to Word with the document to be exported open. From Word save it in \plain \f26 \b RTF\plain \f26  format, preferably with the same name (but extension ".RTF") and in the same directory as the original document.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 Now switch back to RTFMAC and complete the dialog, by selecting the destination disk and folder on the Mac with the lower file selector. The program 'remembers' the destination path you used the previous time. The upper selector specifies the file to be transferred, initialised to the same name as the previously selected Word document with ".RTF" extension.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 When you press \plain \f26 \b Enter\plain \f26  the program copies the RTF file to the specified destination on the Mac, changing the representation of non-ASCII characters as described previously. The file can then be opened from Word on the Mac.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 As a bonus RTFMAC can be made to insert the current date into the document. It can do this in three different formats, corresponding to the three formats used by the Microsoft Word \plain \f26 \b Glossary\plain \f26  command. Use the following place holders in your document to specify the location and format:\par \pard \plain \s19 \qj \fi-1440 \li1440 \sa240 \sl240 \tx1440 \f26 \b \'AEdate\'AF\plain \f26 \tab "Full" format, e.g.: Monday, April 11, 1994;\par \pard \plain \s19 \qj \fi-1440 \li1440 \sa240 \sl240 \tx1440 \f26 \b \'AEadate\'AF\plain \f26 \tab "Abbreviated" format, e.g.: Mon, Apr 11, 1994;\par \pard \plain \s19 \qj \fi-1440 \li1440 \sa240 \sl240 \tx1440 \f26 \b \'AEsdate\'AF\plain \f26 \tab "Short" format, e.g.: 11/4/94.\par \pard \plain \s17 \sb40 \sa280 \sl280 \keepn \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f8 \fs28 \b 5\tab Use and Distribution\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 I am making this software available as freeware, and therefore it may be used free of charge. It may be freely distributed so long as no charge is made (other than to cover reasonable costs of distribution), and these notes are distributed with the programs.\par \pard \plain \s0 \qj \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760 \f26 While I have taken reasonable care in the production of this software, I cannot be held responsible for any loss or damage resulting from its use. If any problems are encountered I will try and assist. I can be contacted at:\par \pard \plain \s20 \li720 \sa240 \sl240 \tx720 \tx1440 \tx2160 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \tx5040 \tx5760